{%- comment -%}
  Product page — first section design. Added by EDD (Aug 2026), revamped Aug 2026.
  Rendered from sections/main-product.liquid, so it only ever loads on a PDP.

  GOAL
  Everything a buyer needs to decide — image, title, rating, price, and BOTH buy
  buttons — usable without scrolling on a 390x844 phone.

  THE REAL FOLD IS 781px, NOT 844.
  .hh-botnav is position:fixed and owns the bottom 63px. Geometry alone will happily
  report an element "above the fold" while the nav sits on top of it, so every number
  below is measured against 781 and confirmed with a screenshot.

  WHAT THE REVAMP CHANGED (measured before -> after, 390x844)
    Gallery image      300px            -> 280px
    Quantity stepper   own row at 669   -> relocated below the buy buttons
    Add To Cart        730-780          -> 643-693
    Buy It Now         790-840 (BEHIND  -> 703-753 (28px clear of the nav)
                       the bottom nav,
                       100% unreachable)
    Floating video     covered 31% of ATC and all of Buy It Now -> yields to both

  Buy It Now was the worst of it: the client asked for it to carry the primary colour,
  and on a phone it was rendering entirely underneath the fixed bottom nav. Colour on an
  invisible button is worth nothing, so the layout had to give it somewhere to live.

  WHY QUANTITY MOVED BELOW THE BUTTONS
  The stepper cost 72px (50px control + 22px gap) directly above the CTA. This is a
  single-title book: nearly every order is one copy, the cart drawer edits quantity in
  one tap, and the theme's own sticky Add To Cart bar carries no quantity control
  either — so treating it as secondary is consistent with the theme, not a break from
  it. It is still on the page, immediately under Buy It Now, and now shows its
  "Quantity" label (the theme hides it) since it no longer sits in an obvious buy row.

  WHY NOT PUT QUANTITY BESIDE ADD TO CART
  Tried and rejected twice. Side-by-side needs the buy block flattened with
  display:contents through .ap-productform__buy-buttons > form > ap-paymentcontainerproduct,
  and that last element is #ApolloProductSticky — the anchor the theme's sticky-CTA
  IntersectionObserver measures. display:contents removes its box, so the observer would
  see a zero-height target and the sticky bar would stop appearing. Not worth it.

  Related changes made outside this file:
  - sections/header-group-top.json: static announcement bar off, `scroll-text` marquee on.
  - sections/main-product.liquid: breadcrumb render commented out; the `text` block
    wrapped in .pdp-short-desc; the `payment` block body replaced with pdp-payment-trust.
  - templates/product.json: vendor, sku, categories, tags, share and product_policy
    blocks disabled.
  - snippets/product-buy.liquid: #AddToCart swapped to button--secondary.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign fs_bp = 767 -%}

<style>
  /* ---------- All widths ---------- */

  /* The free-shipping line duplicated the marquee message directly below the price.
     One statement, in one place — the strip. Scoped to the product form so the cart
     drawer's own shipping progress bar is untouched. */
  .ap-productform .shipping-bar { display: none; }

  /* GALLERY — SQUARE BOX.
     The theme sizes each slide with the padding-top aspect hack, written INLINE per
     slide as style="padding-top:140.0%" (the media's natural 640x896 ratio) with the
     image absolutely positioned inside. Inline styles beat stylesheets, so forcing the
     box square needs !important — there is no class or setting to do it.

     Desktop cost of the natural ratio: .product__media was 1014px tall, so the gallery
     ran ~300px past the entire buy column beside it. At 1:1 the slide is 648x648 and the
     whole media block comes down to ~755px.

     object-fit goes cover -> contain. These are book covers: cropping a 140%-tall image
     into a square would slice ~29% off the top and bottom and take the title with it.
     contain fits the whole cover inside the square and gutters the sides instead. */
  .product__media .ap-product-media-image-wrapper {
    padding-top: 100% !important;
    max-height: none;
  }
  .product__media .ap-product-media-image-wrapper img,
  .product__media .product__media-item-image img {
    object-fit: contain;
  }

  /* The <img> box still fills the whole square; contain letterboxes the painted pixels
     inside it, so the visible cover sits centred with a gutter each side. The thumbnail
     strip is only 372px wide and was left-aligned to the 648px column, which read as
     misaligned against the centred cover.

     It is centred on .product__media-nav (flex, 648px) — margin:auto on the strip itself
     does nothing because every ancestor down to it is also 372px wide.

     Desktop only: on mobile that same .product__media-nav carries the prev/next arrows
     and the dots, laid out edge-to-edge. Centring it there would pull the arrows into
     the middle of the page. */
  @media screen and (min-width: {{ fs_bp | plus: 1 }}px) {
    .product__media-nav { justify-content: center; }
  }

  /* The Save badge shipped as pure rgb(255,0,0). Next to the brand orange on the price
     and on Buy It Now that raw red is the one unbranded colour on the screen, and it
     reads as a sticker rather than a designed element. #C42B1C keeps the badge loud
     enough to be the second thing you see after the price, without putting a third
     competing warm hue beside the CTA. Scoped to the product meta so .label--highlight
     on collection cards is untouched. */
  .ap-productmeta__label-list .label--highlight {
    background-color: #C42B1C;
    border-color: #C42B1C;
  }

  /* Button hierarchy — Buy It Now is the committed action and keeps the primary
     orange; Add To Cart steps back to the theme's secondary.

     Done by swapping the class in snippets/product-buy.liquid, NOT with a colour
     override here. These buttons are painted with `background-image:
     linear-gradient(...)`, so a `background-color` rule applies but is covered by
     the gradient — computed style reports your colour while the pixels stay orange.

     Buy It Now needs no rule at all: its orange comes from the .shopify-payment-button
     WRAPPER, which the theme already colours (the inner button is transparent, and its
     class is `--unbrand`, not `--unbranded`). */

  /* Two fixed widgets sit in the bottom-right corner, both ON TOP of the buy buttons:
       .video-float-preview  Shoppable ShortVid player, z-1000, covered 102px (31%) of
                             Add To Cart and the whole of Buy It Now
       .hh-whatsapp          WhatsApp bubble, z-900, covers 42px (13%) of Buy It Now
     Buy It Now is full-bleed at 330px wide, so there is no corner left to move either
     widget into while the buy block is on screen — the only honest fix is to have them
     yield. Both fade out while the buttons are visible and return the instant they
     scroll away, so each widget still works everywhere except standing on the CTA.

     The video is an app embed and cannot be changed at the source, but its markup sets
     only `display`, leaving opacity and visibility free for us to drive.

     Toggle: Theme settings -> Product page - payment & trust -> "Hide the floating
     video and WhatsApp bubble while the buy buttons are on screen". */
  .video-float-preview,
  .hh-whatsapp {
    transition: opacity .2s ease, visibility .2s ease;
  }
  body.pdp-cta-visible .video-float-preview,
  body.pdp-cta-visible .hh-whatsapp {
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME PROBLEM — the STICKY bar.
     The rule above only covers the buy block in the first screen. Once that scrolls
     away the theme raises the sticky Add To Cart / Buy Now bar, and those same two
     widgets sit straight on top of it. Measured live at 1280x900:

       sticky Buy Now button   x 1147-1265, y 821-869
       .video-float-preview    x 1096-1260, y 588-880   z-1000
       .hh-whatsapp            x 1101-1262, y 826-882   z-900

     A hit test every 2px across the button came back 98% blocked on desktop and 32%
     on a 390px phone. The button was there, painted, and all but unclickable.

     This is not new — the old sticky "Add to cart" button sat in exactly the same
     place under exactly the same widgets. It only became worth fixing when that
     button became the primary way to buy.

     They are LIFTED, not hidden. Hiding them here would mean hiding them for the
     whole product page (the sticky bar shows precisely when the first-screen buttons
     do not), which would switch off a video app the client pays for. Both are
     position:fixed with a `bottom` and no transform of their own, so driving `bottom`
     moves them cleanly and leaves .hh-whatsapp's hover transform alone.

     The offset is the measured bar height, not a guess — the bar is 110px on desktop
     and 74px on a phone, and it is an app-rendered element that can change. */
  .video-float-preview,
  .hh-whatsapp {
    transition: opacity .2s ease, visibility .2s ease, bottom .2s ease;
  }
  body.pdp-sticky-visible .video-float-preview {
    bottom: calc(var(--pdp-sticky-h, 110px) + 20px) !important;
  }
  body.pdp-sticky-visible .hh-whatsapp {
    bottom: calc(var(--pdp-sticky-h, 110px) + 18px) !important;
  }

  /* ---------- Phone ---------- */
  @media screen and (max-width: {{ fs_bp }}px) {

    /* 1. Gallery — see the square-box rules above. On a 330px column a full-width
          square would be 330 tall: 50px more than the 280px box it replaced, which puts
          Buy It Now back under the bottom nav. 285 keeps the vertical footprint the same
          as before the square change and centres the box with a 22px gutter each side.
          Measured: at 300 the clearance under Buy It Now was 8px, which is inside the
          margin a longer product title would eat. At 285 it is 25px. */
    .product__media .ap-product-media-list-wrapper {
      max-width: 285px;
      margin-left: auto;
      margin-right: auto;
    }

    /* 2. Price. .price--large is only a wrapper; the visible type is on the inner
          .price (30px) and .price--compare (14px). At 30px the pair overflowed the
          223px the Save badge leaves and wrapped "Rs." onto its own line. */
    .ap-productmeta__price-list-container { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .ap-productmeta__price-list-container .price { font-size: 22px; }
    .ap-productmeta__price-list-container .price--compare { font-size: 13px; }
    .ap-productmeta__label-list .label { font-size: 11px; }

    /* 3. Vertical rhythm. */
    .ap-productmeta__title {
      margin-bottom: 8px;
      font-size: 20px;
      line-height: 1.25;
    }

    /* 4. Buy before read. Reordering is done here rather than in templates/product.json
          because Shopify's template validator rejects a reordered block_order on this
          template (with a misleading "product-recommendations does not refer to an
          existing section file"). CSS also leaves desktop order intact. */
    .ap-productform {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
    }
    .ap-productform > * { order: 50; }
    .ap-productform > .ap-productmeta__title                { order: 10; }
    .ap-productform > .shopify-block                        { order: 20; }  /* Judge.me rating */
    .ap-productform > .ap-productmeta__price-list-container { order: 30; }
    .ap-productform > .pdp-short-desc                       { order: 60; }
    .ap-productform > .product-policy                       { order: 70; }
    .ap-productform > .ap-productmeta__aside                { order: 99; }

    /* 5. The buy row. .quantity_wrapper goes display:contents so its two children
          become direct flex items of .ap-productform and can be ordered apart —
          buttons first, stepper after. Nothing observes .quantity_wrapper's box, so
          dissolving it is safe (unlike #ApolloProductSticky further down the tree). */
    .quantity_wrapper { display: contents; }

    /* NOTE the missing child combinator on the next two. display:contents makes these
       flex ITEMS of .ap-productform, but selectors still match the DOM tree, where they
       are grandchildren — `.ap-productform > .ap-productform__quantity` matches nothing.
       Getting this wrong silently drops them to order 0 and floats the whole buy block
       above the product title. The full path keeps them scoped to this form. */
    .ap-productform > .quantity_wrapper > .ap-productform__buy-buttons { order: 40; margin-top: 12px; }
    .ap-productform > .quantity_wrapper > .ap-productform__quantity    { order: 45; margin-top: 14px; }
    .ap-productform > .pdp-trust                                       { order: 50; margin-top: 18px; }

    /* The stepper no longer sits in an obvious buy row, so give it back its label.
       sections/main-product.liquid hides it with
       `.ap-productform .quantity_wrapper .ap-productform__quantity-label {display:none!important}`,
       so this needs both !important and a longer selector to win. */
    .ap-productform > .quantity_wrapper > .ap-productform__quantity {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 12px;
    }
    .ap-productform > .quantity_wrapper > .ap-productform__quantity > .ap-productform__quantity-label {
      display: block !important;
      margin-bottom: 0;
      font-size: 13px;
      font-weight: 600;
      color: #3A2E22;
    }
    .ap-quantityselector { height: 44px; }
    .ap-quantityselector__button { width: 42px; height: 44px; }
    .ap-quantityselector__input  { height: 44px; }

    .pdp-short-desc { margin-top: 18px; }
  }
</style>

{%- if settings.pdp_video_yields_to_cta -%}
  <script>
    /* Fades the floating video out while the buy block is on screen. Watches the buy
       buttons, not the video, so it stays correct if the app changes its own markup.
       Everything is feature-detected and wrapped — if any of it is unavailable the
       page is simply left as it was. */
    (function () {
      if (!('IntersectionObserver' in window)) return;
      var start = function () {
        var target = document.querySelector('.ap-productform__buy-buttons');
        if (target) {
          var io = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
            document.body.classList.toggle('pdp-cta-visible', entries[0].isIntersecting);
          }, { threshold: 0 });
          io.observe(target);
        }

        /* Sticky bar: publish its visibility and its measured height, so the widgets
           above it move by exactly as much as they need to. The theme shows and hides
           the bar with the `hidden` attribute, so that is what we watch. */
        var bar = document.querySelector('ap-productstickyform');
        if (!bar || !('MutationObserver' in window)) return;

        var sync = function () {
          var shown = !bar.hasAttribute('hidden');
          document.body.classList.toggle('pdp-sticky-visible', shown);
          if (shown) {
            var h = Math.round(bar.getBoundingClientRect().height);
            if (h > 0) document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--pdp-sticky-h', h + 'px');
          }
        };

        new MutationObserver(sync).observe(bar, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['hidden'] });
        window.addEventListener('resize', sync);
        sync();
      };
      if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
        document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', start);
      } else {
        start();
      }
    })();
  </script>
{%- endif -%}
